---
const formalDefinitions = [
  {
    termHtml: '<span class="math-var">V</span>',
    description: "is the set of certification authorities and governed trust points in the federated ecosystem.",
  },
  {
    termHtml:
      '<span class="math-var">E</span> &sube; <span class="math-var">V</span> &times; <span class="math-var">V</span>',
    description: "is the set of admissible federation relations between certification authorities.",
  },
  {
    termHtml:
      '<span class="math-var">w</span> : <span class="math-var">E</span> &rarr; ℝ<sub>&ge;0</sub>',
    description: "is the edge cost function.",
  },
  {
    termHtml:
      '&Phi; : <span class="math-var">E</span> &rarr; <span class="math-var">C</span>',
    description: "assigns to each edge a set of governance constraints.",
  },
];

const costComponents = [
  {
    termHtml: '<span class="math-var">crypto</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)',
    description: "cryptographic posture of the relation, such as classical, hybrid, or post-quantum.",
  },
  {
    termHtml: '<span class="math-var">alg</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)',
    description: "algorithm family used by the relation, including interoperability implications.",
  },
  {
    termHtml: '<span class="math-var">key</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)',
    description: "key strength or associated technical assurance level.",
  },
  {
    termHtml: '<span class="math-var">dist</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)',
    description: "structural distance introduced when traversing domains, bridges, or governance boundaries.",
  },
];

const governanceComponents = [
  {
    termHtml: '<span class="math-var">prop</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)',
    relationHtml:
      '<span class="math-var">prop</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>) &isin; &#123;none, limited, full&#125;',
    description:
      "This determines whether trust may stop at the current relation, propagate in a bounded way, or be fully reusable across further federation steps.",
  },
  {
    termHtml: '<span class="math-var">depth</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)',
    relationHtml:
      '<span class="math-var">depth</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>) &isin; ℕ',
    description:
      "This bounds how many additional federation steps may be traversed after the relation is used.",
  },
  {
    termHtml: '<span class="math-var">scope</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)',
    description:
      "This specifies the operational context in which the relation may be reused, such as TLS, authentication, code signing, or document signing.",
  },
  {
    termHtml: '<span class="math-var">pol</span><sup>+</sup>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)',
    description:
      "These identify certification policies that are admissible for route construction through the relation.",
  },
  {
    termHtml: '<span class="math-var">pol</span><sup>-</sup>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)',
    description:
      "These identify certification policies that must exclude the relation from candidate paths.",
  },
  {
    termHtml: '<span class="math-var">name</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)',
    description:
      "These restrict the naming space over which trust may apply, for example DNS domains, email namespaces, X.500 subtrees, or specific organizations.",
  },
  {
    termHtml: '<span class="math-var">ku</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)',
    description:
      "These restrict which key usage semantics remain valid when traversing the relation.",
  },
  {
    termHtml: '<span class="math-var">eku</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)',
    description:
      "These restrict which application-level purposes remain admissible, such as server authentication or code signing.",
  },
  {
    termHtml: '<span class="math-var">time</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)',
    relationHtml:
      '<span class="math-var">time</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>) = [<span class="math-var">t</span><sub>from</sub>(<span class="math-var">e</span>), <span class="math-var">t</span><sub>to</sub>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)]',
    description:
      "This expresses the governance time interval during which the trust relation is considered admissible, independently of whether underlying certificates remain syntactically valid under PKIX.",
  },
];

const admissibleConditions = [
  "trust transitivity rules,",
  "maximum permitted propagation depth,",
  "required functional scope,",
  "allowed and non-denied certification policies,",
  "namespace restrictions,",
  "Key Usage and Extended Key Usage restrictions,",
  "and the governed trust validity window.",
];
---

<section id="research" class="paper-section">
  <div class="container paper-shell">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="section-kicker">Research</span>
      <h2>Formalizing federated PKI as a governed weighted graph.</h2>
      <p>
        The proposal introduces a formal layer above PKIX semantics, representing
        certification authorities and governed federation relationships as a directed,
        weighted graph that can be queried for valid and optimal certification paths.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="research-grid">
      <article class="research-card">
        <div class="research-card-header">
          <h3>Governed trust graph</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          Instead of inheriting trust from entire hierarchies, PolarisChain models
          trust as explicit graph edges between governed certification authorities.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Vertices represent trust anchors, bridge CAs, issuing CAs, and other governed trust points.</li>
          <li>Edges encode admissible inter-domain relationships instead of broad hierarchy inheritance.</li>
          <li>The model preserves PKIX validation while making federation semantics explicit and queryable.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="research-card">
        <div class="research-card-header">
          <h3>Optimal path semantics</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          The proposal defines a multicriteria cost over federation edges so path
          selection becomes an optimization problem rather than a heuristic search.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Edge weights capture cryptographic profile, algorithm family, key strength, and structural distance.</li>
          <li>Dijkstra-based traversal computes a deterministic minimum-cost route over admissible paths.</li>
          <li>The result is an explainable certification chain between issuer-side entities and governed trust points.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="model-section" aria-labelledby="mathematical-model-heading">
      <div class="model-heading">
        <span class="note-label">Mathematical model</span>
        <h3 id="mathematical-model-heading">A compact formalization of governed federation and path cost.</h3>
        <p>
          PKIX certificate validation remains intact. The graph layer makes admissible
          trust propagation explicit, separates technical edge cost from hard
          governance constraints, and selects routes only among policy-compliant
          federation paths.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="model-grid">
        <article class="research-card model-story-card">
          <div class="model-story-flow">
            <section class="model-step" aria-labelledby="model-formal-definition">
              <div class="model-step-header model-step-header-cost">
                <span class="model-step-marker">01</span>
                <h4 id="model-formal-definition">Formal definition</h4>
                <p>
                  The federated PKI ecosystem is modeled as a governed weighted graph:
                </p>
                <div class="model-math-display" aria-label="G equals open parenthesis V comma E comma w comma Phi close parenthesis">
                  <p class="math-line math-line-display">
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">G</span> = (<span class="math-var">V</span>, <span class="math-var">E</span>, <span class="math-var">w</span>, &Phi;)</span>
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="model-step-body">
                <p class="model-step-label">where:</p>
                <dl class="model-definition-list">
                  {formalDefinitions.map((item) => (
                    <div class="model-definition-item">
                      <dt>
                        <span class="model-definition-term" set:html={item.termHtml}></span>
                      </dt>
                      <dd>{item.description}</dd>
                    </div>
                  ))}
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>

            <section class="model-step model-step-cost" aria-labelledby="model-cost-function">
              <div class="model-step-header">
                <span class="model-step-marker">02</span>
                <h4 id="model-cost-function">Cost function</h4>
                <p>
                  As a natural evolution of the original formulation, technical and
                  structural cost remains explicitly separated from governance
                  constraints:
                </p>
                <div class="model-math-display" aria-label="w of e equals f of crypto of e, alg of e, key of e, dist of e">
                  <p class="math-line math-line-display">
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">w</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>) = <span class="math-var">f</span>(</span>
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">crypto</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>),</span>
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">alg</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>),</span>
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">key</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>),</span>
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">dist</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>))</span>
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="model-step-body">
                <p class="model-step-label">where:</p>
                <dl class="model-definition-list">
                  {costComponents.map((item) => (
                    <div class="model-definition-item">
                      <dt>
                        <span class="model-definition-term" set:html={item.termHtml}></span>
                      </dt>
                      <dd>{item.description}</dd>
                    </div>
                  ))}
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>

            <section class="model-step" aria-labelledby="model-governance-per-edge">
              <div class="model-step-header">
                <span class="model-step-marker">03</span>
                <h4 id="model-governance-per-edge">Governance constraints<br />per edge</h4>
                <p>
                  For each edge <span class="math-var">e</span> &isin; <span class="math-var">E</span>, the governance layer defines the hard constraints that determine whether trust may propagate through that relation at all:
                </p>
                <div class="model-math-display" aria-label="Phi of e equals trans of e, depth of e, scope of e, pol plus of e, pol minus of e, name of e, ku of e, eku of e, time of e">
                  <p class="math-line math-line-display">
                    <span class="math-piece">&Phi;(<span class="math-var">e</span>) = (<span class="math-var">prop</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>), <span class="math-var">depth</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>), <span class="math-var">scope</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>),</span>
                  </p>
                  <p class="math-line math-line-display math-line-indent">
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">pol</span><sup>+</sup>(<span class="math-var">e</span>), <span class="math-var">pol</span><sup>-</sup>(<span class="math-var">e</span>), <span class="math-var">name</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>), <span class="math-var">ku</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>), <span class="math-var">eku</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>), <span class="math-var">time</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>))</span>
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="model-step-body">
                <p class="model-step-label">where:</p>
                <dl class="model-definition-list model-definition-list-governance">
                  {governanceComponents.map((item) => (
                    <div class="model-definition-item model-definition-item-governance">
                      <dt>
                        <span class="model-definition-term" set:html={item.termHtml}></span>
                        {item.relationHtml && (
                          <span class="model-definition-relation" set:html={item.relationHtml}></span>
                        )}
                      </dt>
                      <dd>{item.description}</dd>
                    </div>
                  ))}
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>

            <section class="model-step" aria-labelledby="model-admissible-paths">
              <div class="model-step-header">
                <span class="model-step-marker">04</span>
                <h4 id="model-admissible-paths">Admissible paths</h4>
                <p>For a candidate path:</p>
                <div class="model-math-display" aria-label="P equals e 1, e 2, ellipsis, e k">
                  <p class="math-line math-line-display">
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">P</span> = (</span>
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">e</span><sub>1</sub>,</span>
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">e</span><sub>2</sub>,</span>
                    <span class="math-piece">&hellip;,</span>
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">e</span><sub>k</sub>)</span>
                  </p>
                </div>
                <p class="model-step-text">the set of admissible paths is defined as:</p>
                <div class="model-math-display" aria-label="P adm equals set of P in script P such that P satisfies Phi">
                  <p class="math-line math-line-display">
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">P</span><sub>adm</sub> = &#123;</span>
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">P</span> &isin; 𝒫</span>
                    <span class="math-piece">|</span>
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">P</span> &#8872; &Phi;</span>
                    <span class="math-piece">&#125;</span>
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="model-step-body">
                <p class="model-step-text">
                  Here, <span class="math-var">P</span> &#8872; &Phi; means that the path satisfies all governance constraints simultaneously.
                </p>
                <ol class="model-condition-list">
                  {admissibleConditions.map((item) => (
                    <li>{item}</li>
                  ))}
                </ol>
              </div>
            </section>

            <section class="model-step" aria-labelledby="model-optimal-path">
              <div class="model-step-header">
                <span class="model-step-marker">05</span>
                <h4 id="model-optimal-path">Optimal path</h4>
                <p>
                  The optimal route is therefore not computed over all connected
                  paths, but only over those that are admissible under governance
                  policy:
                </p>
                <div class="model-math-display" aria-label="P star equals arg min over P in P adm of sum over e in P of w of e">
                  <p class="math-line math-line-display">
                    <span class="math-piece"><span class="math-var">P</span><sup>*</sup> = arg min<sub><span class="math-var">P</span> &isin; <span class="math-var">P</span><sub>adm</sub></sub></span>
                  </p>
                  <p class="math-line math-line-display math-line-indent">
                    <span class="math-piece">&Sigma;<sub><span class="math-var">e</span> &isin; <span class="math-var">P</span></sub> <span class="math-var">w</span>(<span class="math-var">e</span>)</span>
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="model-step-body">
                <p class="model-step-text">
                  This formulation ensures that non-admissible relations do not compete as merely expensive alternatives. They are excluded from the search space altogether. Route optimization therefore occurs only after governance, scope, policy, and propagation constraints have already been enforced.
                </p>
                <p class="model-step-conclusion">
                  PKIX validation semantics remain unchanged. The graph layer does not replace certificate validation; it governs admissible trust propagation and computes the minimum-cost route only across policy-compliant federation paths.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="research-notes">
      <div>
        <span class="note-label">Research problem</span>
        <p>
          Certification path discovery becomes increasingly difficult as multidomain PKI federations grow denser.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="note-label">Methodological lens</span>
        <p>
          Directed weighted graphs, deterministic shortest-path computation, and policy-aware trust modelling.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div id="interoperability">
        <span class="note-label">Interoperability objective</span>
        <p>
          Support controlled interoperability across classical, hybrid, and post-quantum PKI domains.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
